cixfan-dkms (1.1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Verify that the fan is really under our control, and hand it back to the
    firmware otherwise.  Fan profile byte 0 is undocumented, so on an EC
    firmware that numbers its profiles differently the commands could be
    accepted while the duty writes were ignored, leaving the driver running a
    curve it was not actually driving.
  * At probe, step the duty and read it back, refusing to bind if the EC does
    not report what was asked for.  This proves the command path, but cannot
    prove that profile 0 means manual: measured on this board, an EC left in
    automatic mode held a manual duty write untouched for 45 seconds, and
    re-sending a profile does not force a re-evaluation.
  * So also check continuously, before each re-assert, and give the fan back
    after three consecutive disagreements.  That is what catches an EC which
    accepts control and then fights for it, since the difference is only
    visible when the EC re-evaluates its own target.
  * Add the skip_probe_check module parameter for an EC that accepts control
    but cannot report its duty back.  It does not disable the runtime check.

 -- Roy Kaandorp <roykaandorp@gmail.com>  Sun, 30 Aug 2026 10:05:00 +0200

cixfan-dkms (1.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Initial release.
  * Restore numeric Chrome EC fan control under ACPI by installing an ACPI
    passthrough method over the firmware's \_SB.EC0.TRAS transport.
  * Select the EC's undocumented manual fan profile
    (EC_CMD_THERMAL_AUTO_FAN_CTRL byte 0) so written duties are not
    overwritten by the EC's own control loop.
  * Drive a quiet curve off the SoC thermal zones, with handback to the
    firmware profile on unload, reboot, shutdown, suspend, repeated EC
    errors and an emergency temperature.

 -- Roy Kaandorp <roykaandorp@gmail.com>  Sun, 30 Aug 2026 03:30:00 +0200
